  "hints": [
    "18,000 rps of the traffic are images. Don't let them touch your servers.",
    "Feeds are read far more than written and celebrity posts are extremely hot: cache feeds. Posting should only enqueue a fan-out job.",
    "Client \u2192 CDN, and Client \u2192 LB (3) \u2192 Feed Service (12) \u2192 Cache (2 \u00d7 32 GB) and NoSQL (2). Feed Service \u2192 Queue \u2192 Fan-out Workers (2) \u2192 NoSQL."
  ],
  "scoring": {
    "weights": {
      "performance": 0.35,
      "reliability": 0.3,
      "cost": 0.15,
      "architecture": 0.2
    },
    "latencyTargetMs": 40,
    "costBudget": 2000
  },
  "explanation": [
    "Fan-out on write: when someone posts, workers push the post id into each follower's precomputed timeline, so reading a feed is a single cheap lookup.",
    "Celebrities break fan-out on write (writing to 50M timelines per post), so real systems mix strategies: fan out for normal users, and merge celebrity posts in at read time.",
    "Images are by far the most bytes. A CDN serves them from the edge, and the originals live in object storage.",
    "A feed cache with high hot-key affinity absorbs most reads, which lets a modest NoSQL cluster handle the rest."
  ]
}
